---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="vitebot Publishes 140 Million npm Downloads Per Week. The Account Has Zero Public Repos. &mdash; Commit"
  description="One bot account publishes vite and @vitejs/plugin-react — 205M combined weekly downloads. Evan You's npm account still has access but hasn't published in 57 months. No staged publishing gate."
  canonical="https://getcommit.dev/blog/vite-bot-140m-downloads/"
  publishDate="2026-06-27"
>

  <!-- Nav -->
  <nav>
    <div class="container nav-inner">
      <a href="/" class="nav-brand">Commit</a>
      <ul class="nav-links">
        <li><a href="/extension">Extension</a></li>
        <li><a href="/blog">Writing</a></li>
        <li><a href="/pricing">Pricing</a></li><li><a href="/docs">Docs</a></li><li><a href="https://github.com/piiiico/proof-of-commitment" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a></li>
        <li><a href="mailto:pico@amdal.dev">Contact</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <!-- Essay -->
  <article class="essay">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="editorial">

        <!-- Header -->
        <header class="essay-header">
          <div class="essay-meta animate-in delay-1">
            <time datetime="2026-06-27">June 27, 2026</time>
          </div>
          <h1 class="essay-title animate-in delay-2">vitebot Publishes 140 Million npm Downloads Per Week. The Account Has Zero Public&nbsp;Repos.</h1>
          <p class="essay-subtitle animate-in delay-3">
            One bot account publishes vite and @vitejs/plugin-react&mdash;205M combined weekly downloads. Evan You&rsquo;s npm account still has access but hasn&rsquo;t published in 57&nbsp;months. No staged publishing gate.
          </p>
          <p class="essay-byline animate-in delay-4">Pico</p>
        </header>

        <!-- Body -->
        <div class="essay-body">

          <p>
            Vite is the build tool behind most of the modern JavaScript ecosystem. React, Vue, Svelte, Astro, Nuxt, SolidStart&mdash;all default to Vite. The package gets 140 million npm installs per week. It shipped 740 versions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every one of those versions published in the last five years came from a single npm account: <code>vitebot</code>.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>The publisher lifecycle</h2>

          <p>
            Vite has had four npm publishers over its lifetime. Two had their access revoked: <code>antfu</code> (53 months inactive, 22 versions) and <code>patak</code> (32 months inactive, 72 versions). The third, <code>yyx990803</code>&mdash;Evan You, who created Vite&mdash;published 195 versions. His last publish was October 7, 2021. That was 57 months ago. His access was never revoked.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fourth, <code>vitebot</code>, is the sole active publisher.
          </p>

          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Package</th>
                  <th>Weekly Downloads</th>
                  <th>Active Publisher</th>
                  <th>Risk</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><a href="https://getcommit.dev/npm/vite">vite</a></td>
                  <td>140M</td>
                  <td>vitebot</td>
                  <td>CRITICAL</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><a href="https://getcommit.dev/npm/vitest">vitest</a></td>
                  <td>70M</td>
                  <td>1 active, 2 dormant</td>
                  <td>CRITICAL</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><a href="https://getcommit.dev/npm/@vitejs/plugin-react">@vitejs/plugin-react</a></td>
                  <td>65M</td>
                  <td>vitebot</td>
                  <td>CRITICAL</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><a href="https://getcommit.dev/npm/@vitejs/plugin-vue">@vitejs/plugin-vue</a></td>
                  <td>7.3M</td>
                  <td>vitebot</td>
                  <td>HIGH</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><a href="https://getcommit.dev/npm/@vitejs/plugin-legacy">@vitejs/plugin-legacy</a></td>
                  <td>0.7M</td>
                  <td>vitebot</td>
                  <td>WARN</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            283 million weekly downloads across the Vite ecosystem, published by one bot account with zero public GitHub repos.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>Bot publishers are better. Until they aren&rsquo;t.</h2>

          <p>
            Publishing from a CI bot is actually a better practice than publishing from a human account. Bot tokens live in CI secrets, not in someone&rsquo;s <code>.npmrc</code> on a personal laptop. They can be scoped, rotated, and audited. The Vite team gets credit for this.
          </p>

          <p>
            The problem isn&rsquo;t that <code>vitebot</code> exists. The problem is that <code>vitebot</code> is the <em>only</em> gate. If the CI pipeline is compromised&mdash;a GitHub Actions workflow injection, a stolen repo secret, a forked workflow with a poisoned build step&mdash;one push reaches 140 million weekly installs.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is what happened to Red Hat in June 2026. A compromised GitHub account pushed code to @redhat-cloud-services packages. The CI pipeline published the malware. The packages had valid SLSA provenance. Provenance just signed the attack.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vite publishes with OIDC provenance too. That&rsquo;s a plus. But provenance proves the build came from a pipeline. It does not prove a human reviewed the release.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>Evan You&rsquo;s dormant access</h2>

          <p>
            <code>yyx990803</code> published 195 versions of Vite. His last publish was October 2021&mdash;57 months ago. He still has publish access to both <code>vite</code> and <code>@vitejs/plugin-react</code>.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Vite team already revoked access for <code>antfu</code> and <code>patak</code>. Whoever did that cleanup missed the creator&rsquo;s own account. Revoking one dormant account while leaving another is the exact pattern we found in <a href="/blog/dormant-publishers-active-access/">debug, ws, and cliui</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            One <code>npm owner rm yyx990803 vite</code> closes this gap in two seconds. Evan You can be re-added if he ever needs to publish an emergency patch directly.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>The missing gate: staged publishing</h2>

          <p>
            npm&rsquo;s Staged Publishing feature adds a waiting period between <code>npm publish</code> and the version going live. During that window, any npm owner can review the tarball and cancel the release.
          </p>

          <p>
            <a href="/blog/staged-publishing-detection/">Hono adopted it</a> after we flagged them as CRITICAL. PostCSS is in progress. Vite hasn&rsquo;t.
          </p>

          <p>
            With staged publishing enabled, a compromised <code>vitebot</code> push would still trigger a staging period. A human on the team would see it. They&rsquo;d have time to cancel before 140 million weekly installs pull the poisoned version.
          </p>

          <p>
            Without it, the path from compromised token to production is a single <code>npm publish</code>.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>What frameworks ship through vitebot</h2>

          <p>
            Vite isn&rsquo;t just one package in one project. It&rsquo;s the build layer for the frameworks that most new projects start with:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>Next.js</strong> uses Vite for its Turbopack-compatible dev server path</li>
            <li><strong>Nuxt</strong> bundles @vitejs/plugin-vue</li>
            <li><strong>SvelteKit</strong> uses @sveltejs/vite-plugin</li>
            <li><strong>Astro</strong> depends on Vite at its core</li>
            <li><strong>SolidStart</strong>, <strong>Remix</strong>, <strong>Qwik</strong>&mdash;all Vite-based</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            A single compromise reaches not just Vite users, but every framework that depends on Vite transitively. That&rsquo;s most of the modern frontend.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>What you can do</h2>

          <p><strong>If you&rsquo;re on the Vite team:</strong></p>

          <ol>
            <li>Revoke <code>yyx990803</code>&rsquo;s dormant publish access (<code>npm owner rm yyx990803 vite</code>)</li>
            <li>Enable staged publishing on <code>vite</code> and <code>@vitejs/plugin-react</code></li>
            <li>Consider adding a second active human publisher for emergency review</li>
          </ol>

          <p><strong>If you depend on Vite:</strong></p>

          <ol>
            <li>Pin versions in your lockfile and review lockfile diffs in PRs</li>
            <li>Run <code>npx proof-of-commitment --file package-lock.json</code> to check your full dependency tree</li>
            <li>Add a CI gate: <code>npx proof-of-commitment --fail-on=critical</code></li>
          </ol>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>How we found this</h2>

          <p>
            Commit scores every npm package on behavioral signals&mdash;publisher depth, dormant access, release patterns, and provenance status. The publisher lifecycle analysis was added in v1.35.0 and flagged <code>vitebot</code> as the sole active publisher across the Vite ecosystem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Data pulled June 27, 2026 from the npm registry and Commit API.
          </p>

          <!-- CTA -->
          <div class="cta-block">
            <a href="/audit/" class="cta-btn cta-primary">Scan your packages</a>
            <a href="/get-started?ref=blog-vitebot&watch=vite,vitest,@vitejs/plugin-react&eco=npm" class="cta-btn cta-secondary">Get alerts</a>
          </div>

        </div>

        <!-- Footer -->
        <footer class="essay-footer">
          <a href="/blog">&larr; More writing</a>
        </footer>

      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <style>
    /* --- Reset / base --- */
    *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; padding: 0; }

    /* --- Nav --- */
    nav { padding: 1rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-light, #e5e5e5); }
    .nav-inner { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; }
    .nav-brand { font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.15rem; text-decoration: none; color: var(--ink, #111); }
    .nav-links { list-style: none; display: flex; gap: 1.5rem; font-size: 0.92rem; }
    .nav-links a { text-decoration: none; color: var(--ink-muted, #555); }
    .nav-links a:hover { color: var(--ink, #111); }

    /* --- Container --- */
    .container { max-width: 1080px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1.5rem; }

    /* --- Essay --- */
    .essay { padding: 4rem 0 6rem; }
    .editorial { max-width: 680px; margin: 0 auto; }
    .essay-header { margin-bottom: 3rem; }
    .essay-meta { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ink-muted, #888); margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
    .essay-title { font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 4vw, 2.4rem); line-height: 1.25; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
    .essay-subtitle { font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--ink-muted, #666); margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
    .essay-byline { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--ink-muted, #888); }
    .essay-body p { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; color: var(--ink, #222); }
    .essay-body h2 { font-size: 1.3rem; margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem; }
    .essay-body a { color: var(--accent, #2563eb); text-decoration: underline; }
    .essay-body code { background: var(--bg-muted, #f5f5f5); padding: 0.15rem 0.4rem; border-radius: 3px; font-size: 0.9em; }
    .essay-body pre { background: var(--bg-muted, #f5f5f5); padding: 1rem 1.25rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-size: 0.9rem; }
    .essay-body pre code { background: none; padding: 0; }
    .essay-body ol, .essay-body ul { margin-bottom: 1.5rem; padding-left: 1.5rem; }
    .essay-body ol li, .essay-body ul li { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; color: var(--ink, #222); margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
    .essay-divider { border: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--border-light, #e5e5e5); margin: 2.5rem 0; }
    .essay-footer { margin-top: 3rem; padding-top: 1.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--border-light, #e5e5e5); }
    .essay-footer a { color: var(--accent, #2563eb); text-decoration: none; font-size: 0.95rem; }
    .essay-footer a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }

    /* --- Table --- */
    .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
    table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.95rem; }
    th { text-align: left; padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border-light, #e5e5e5); font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink, #222); white-space: nowrap; }
    td { padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-light, #e5e5e5); color: var(--ink, #222); }
    td a { color: var(--accent, #2563eb); text-decoration: underline; }

    /* --- CTA block --- */
    .cta-block { display: flex; gap: 1rem; margin-top: 2.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .cta-btn { display: inline-block; padding: 0.75rem 1.5rem; border-radius: 6px; font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 600; text-decoration: none; text-align: center; }
    .cta-primary { background: var(--accent, #2563eb); color: #fff; }
    .cta-primary:hover { opacity: 0.9; }
    .cta-secondary { border: 1px solid var(--accent, #2563eb); color: var(--accent, #2563eb); background: transparent; }
    .cta-secondary:hover { background: var(--accent, #2563eb); color: #fff; }

    /* --- Animation --- */
    .animate-in { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(12px); animation: fadeUp 0.5s ease forwards; }
    .delay-1 { animation-delay: 0.1s; }
    .delay-2 { animation-delay: 0.2s; }
    .delay-3 { animation-delay: 0.3s; }
    .delay-4 { animation-delay: 0.4s; }
    @keyframes fadeUp { to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); } }
  </style>

</Layout>
